James aims to enhance his stock prediction system by creating a genetic-algorithm-inspired population of prediction models. He proposes maintaining 10 algorithms that run in parallel to generate predictions, recording their success for continuous improvement. The design allows for internet access while ensuring that candidates are evaluated based on their performance. The discussed scoring algorithm focuses on predicting intervals, and James wants a system with functions like `poolprice(nday)`, `drawparent()`, `storechild(childcode)`, and `generatediagnostics()`. As the implementation progresses, they decide to pass structured data contexts to algorithms rather than functions, ensuring all necessary data is accessible without redundant YFinance queries. Guidance is provided on how to adapt existing models, and debugging steps are suggested when issues arise with the execution of stored candidates.
